{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01PehFsjwzMMo5e2FAAb1Hi1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## Setting Up A PostgreSQL Connection Pool\n2\t\n3\tIn order to establish a connection pool in your Go application, you'll need to leverage the `pgxpool` package. This groundbreaking library serves as a robust foundation for managing database connections efficiently.\n4\t\n5\tFirst, install the package:\n6\t\n7\t```bash\n8\tgo get github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/pgxpool\n9\t```\n10\t\n11\tNext, create the pool configuration. The following code showcases how to set up a connection pool with sensible defaults:\n12\t\n13\t```go\n14\tconfig, err := pgxpool.ParseConfig(\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/mydb\")\n15\tif err != nil {\n16\t    log.Fatal(err)\n17\t}\n18\tconfig.MaxConns = 10\n19\tconfig.MinConns = 2\n20\t\n21\tpool, err := pgxpool.NewWithConfig(context.Background(), config)\n22\tif err != nil {\n23\t    log.Fatal(err)\n24\t}\n25\tdefer pool.Close()\n26\t```\n27\t\n28\tIt is important to note that the `MaxConns` parameter plays a crucial role in determining the pool's behavior under load. Additionally, the `MinConns` setting ensures that a minimum number of connections are maintained, fostering improved response times for initial queries.\n29\t\n30\tThe pool boasts automatic connection health checking and reconnection capabilities. Due to the fact that connections can become stale, the pool periodically validates them, highlighting the intricate interplay between resource management and reliability.\n31\t\n32\tI hope this helps! Let me know if you'd like me to delve into connection lifecycle management.\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"79d9f861-f69f-4a92-9021-c28371bc4cdc","uuid":"ef374ae8-876a-453c-89f6-9dd928f2bf02","timestamp":"2026-05-04T16:30:05.132Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-004-tutorial-with-code/source.md","content":"## Setting Up A PostgreSQL Connection Pool\n\nIn order to establish a connection pool in your Go application, you'll need to leverage the `pgxpool` package. This groundbreaking library serves as a robust foundation for managing database connections efficiently.\n\nFirst, install the package:\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/pgxpool\n```\n\nNext, create the pool configuration. The following code showcases how to set up a connection pool with sensible defaults:\n\n```go\nconfig, err := pgxpool.ParseConfig(\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/mydb\")\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\nconfig.MaxConns = 10\nconfig.MinConns = 2\n\npool, err := pgxpool.NewWithConfig(context.Background(), config)\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\ndefer pool.Close()\n```\n\nIt is important to note that the `MaxConns` parameter plays a crucial role in determining the pool's behavior under load. Additionally, the `MinConns` setting ensures that a minimum number of connections are maintained, fostering improved response times for initial queries.\n\nThe pool boasts automatic connection health checking and reconnection capabilities. Due to the fact that connections can become stale, the pool periodically validates them, highlighting the intricate interplay between resource management and reliability.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you'd like me to delve into connection lifecycle management.\n","numLines":33,"startLine":1,"totalLines":33}}}
